1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,083
Welcome to the Ethereum Speed Run.

2
00:00:03,033 --> 00:00:10,885
In five minutes, you will understand Ethereum end-to-end — from creating a wallet to executing smart contracts on the EVM.

3
00:00:10,885 --> 00:00:17,109
Ethereum was proposed in 2013 by Vitalik Buterin and launched in 2015.

4
00:00:17,109 --> 00:00:22,604
Its purpose is broader than digital money: a global, programmable settlement layer.

5
00:00:22,604 --> 00:00:33,528
On Ethereum, developers can create applications, financial protocols, tokens, NFTs, DAOs, and digital ownership systems without asking permission from a central platform.

6
00:00:33,528 --> 00:00:36,315
The native asset is ETH.

7
00:00:36,315 --> 00:00:45,364
ETH has three main functions: pay for network execution, secure the network through staking, and serve as the economic asset of the ecosystem.

8
00:00:45,364 --> 00:00:48,398
Every transaction consumes gas.

9
00:00:48,398 --> 00:00:51,367
Gas measures computational work.

10
00:00:51,367 --> 00:00:55,312
The user pays gas used multiplied by gas price.

11
00:00:55,312 --> 00:00:59,270
Part of the fee is burned through EIP-1559.

12
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:04,361
The remaining priority fee can go to the validator who includes the transaction.

13
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:06,901
There are no miners anymore.

14
00:01:06,901 --> 00:01:11,822
Since The Merge in 2022, Ethereum uses Proof of Stake.

15
00:01:11,822 --> 00:01:14,934
But how does a user enter this network?

16
00:01:14,934 --> 00:01:17,578
Next episode: wallets.

17
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:21,601
To use Ethereum, you need a cryptographic identity.

18
00:01:21,601 --> 00:01:25,442
It starts with a randomly generated private key.

19
00:01:25,442 --> 00:01:27,552
The flow is simple.

20
00:01:27,552 --> 00:01:30,442
A wallet generates a private key.

21
00:01:30,442 --> 00:01:33,580
The private key derives a public key.

22
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:36,002
The public key is hashed.

23
00:01:36,002 --> 00:01:39,726
The last twenty bytes become an Ethereum address.

24
00:01:39,726 --> 00:01:43,177
That is why addresses start with zero x.

25
00:01:43,177 --> 00:01:45,481
The private key signs.

26
00:01:45,481 --> 00:01:48,033
The address identifies.

27
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:55,039
Ethereum traditionally uses the elliptic curve secp256k1, the same curve used by Bitcoin.

28
00:01:55,039 --> 00:02:01,679
But Ethereum has two main account types: an externally owned account, or EOA, and a contract account.

29
00:02:01,679 --> 00:02:05,026
An EOA is controlled by a private key.

30
00:02:05,026 --> 00:02:09,062
A contract account is controlled by code deployed on-chain.

31
00:02:09,062 --> 00:02:13,815
Your wallet, such as MetaMask, usually manages an EOA.

32
00:02:13,815 --> 00:02:19,062
This account can hold ETH, tokens, NFTs, and send transactions.

33
00:02:19,062 --> 00:02:21,783
But an address alone does nothing.

34
00:02:21,783 --> 00:02:25,638
To change Ethereum's state, you need a transaction.

35
00:02:25,638 --> 00:02:28,645
Next episode: transactions.

36
00:02:28,645 --> 00:02:33,750
An Ethereum transaction is a signed instruction that changes the network state.

37
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:36,653
Its lifecycle is straightforward.

38
00:02:36,653 --> 00:02:39,062
You build a transaction.

39
00:02:39,062 --> 00:02:41,679
Sign it with your private key.

40
00:02:41,679 --> 00:02:44,830
Broadcast it to the peer-to-peer network.

41
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:47,096
It enters the mempool.

42
00:02:47,096 --> 00:02:50,065
A validator includes it in a block.

43
00:02:50,065 --> 00:02:53,893
Every node re-executes and verifies the result.

44
00:02:53,893 --> 00:03:03,190
A transaction can send ETH, transfer an ERC-20 token, mint an NFT, deploy a contract, or call a function in an existing contract.

45
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:12,903
A typical transaction includes a destination address, the ETH being sent, function call data, a nonce, a gas limit, and a maximum fee per gas.

46
00:03:12,903 --> 00:03:17,929
The nonce prevents replay and establishes transaction order for each account.

47
00:03:17,929 --> 00:03:21,080
Gas prevents infinite loops and spam.

48
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:25,468
If execution runs out of gas, the state changes revert.

49
00:03:25,468 --> 00:03:28,203
But the gas spent is still paid.

50
00:03:28,203 --> 00:03:32,408
Now, who chooses which transactions enter the next block?

51
00:03:32,408 --> 00:03:35,273
Next episode: consensus.

52
00:03:35,273 --> 00:03:39,726
Ethereum creates a new block approximately every twelve seconds.

53
00:03:39,726 --> 00:03:47,109
The network is organized into two connected layers: the Execution Layer validates transactions and runs the EVM.

54
00:03:47,109 --> 00:03:51,380
The Consensus Layer coordinates validators and finality.

55
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:55,403
Validators stake ETH to help secure the network.

56
00:03:55,403 --> 00:03:58,919
A validator is selected to propose a block.

57
00:03:58,919 --> 00:04:03,046
Other validators verify it and submit attestations.

58
00:04:03,046 --> 00:04:07,083
Ethereum uses a Proof of Stake system called Gasper.

59
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:10,416
Casper FFG provides finality.

60
00:04:10,416 --> 00:04:14,127
LMD GHOST helps choose the canonical chain.

61
00:04:14,127 --> 00:04:16,927
Time is divided into slots.

62
00:04:16,927 --> 00:04:19,765
Thirty-two slots form an epoch.

63
00:04:19,765 --> 00:04:24,361
After enough validator attestations, blocks become finalized.

64
00:04:24,361 --> 00:04:30,638
Finalized means reversing them would require an economically catastrophic attack against the network.

65
00:04:30,638 --> 00:04:34,466
Validators who behave honestly receive rewards.

66
00:04:34,466 --> 00:04:41,914
Validators who act maliciously, or remain offline for too long, can lose part of their stake through slashing or penalties.

67
00:04:41,914 --> 00:04:45,611
Consensus decides the valid order of blocks.

68
00:04:45,611 --> 00:04:49,635
But execution is where Ethereum becomes programmable.

69
00:04:49,635 --> 00:04:53,593
Final episode: the EVM and smart contracts.

70
00:04:53,593 --> 00:05:00,078
The Ethereum Virtual Machine, or EVM, is the global computation environment of Ethereum.

71
00:05:00,292 --> 00:05:05,684
Every Ethereum node executes the same contract code and reaches the same result.

72
00:05:05,634 --> 00:05:10,126
This is what makes Ethereum a decentralized world computer.

73
00:05:10,126 --> 00:05:14,501
Smart contracts are programs stored at an Ethereum address.

74
00:05:14,501 --> 00:05:20,309
They contain code, persistent state, functions, and rules that execute automatically.

75
00:05:20,309 --> 00:05:23,577
Most contracts are written in Solidity.

76
00:05:23,577 --> 00:05:27,001
A simple example is the Flipper contract.

77
00:05:27,001 --> 00:05:34,931
It stores a boolean value, exposes a function that inverts it, and exposes a read-only function that returns the current state.

78
00:05:34,931 --> 00:05:38,343
When deployed with true, get returns true.

79
00:05:38,343 --> 00:05:41,975
Calling flip changes the stored value to false.

80
00:05:41,975 --> 00:05:45,192
Calling it again changes it back to true.

81
00:05:45,192 --> 00:05:50,009
Get only reads state, so it can be called without sending a transaction.

82
00:05:50,009 --> 00:05:54,540
Calling flip changes Ethereum's global state and requires gas.

83
00:05:54,540 --> 00:06:07,027
This programming model enabled ERC-20 tokens, NFTs through ERC-721, DeFi protocols, stablecoins, decentralized exchanges, DAOs, and on-chain games and identity systems.

84
00:06:07,027 --> 00:06:11,910
Ethereum Layer 1 prioritizes security and decentralization.

85
00:06:11,910 --> 00:06:21,103
For lower fees and higher throughput, activity increasingly happens on Layer 2 networks such as Optimism, Arbitrum, Base, and zero-knowledge rollups.

86
00:06:21,103 --> 00:06:27,431
These layers execute transactions more cheaply, then anchor security and data back to Ethereum.

87
00:06:27,431 --> 00:06:36,415
With Pectra, Ethereum also expanded account-abstraction capabilities, allowing regular accounts to temporarily use smart-contract logic.

88
00:06:36,415 --> 00:06:49,514
Now you understand Ethereum end-to-end: cryptographic wallets and accounts, signed transactions and gas, blocks and Proof of Stake, validators and finality, EVM execution, smart contracts, and Layer 2 scaling.

89
00:06:49,514 --> 00:06:53,290
Ethereum is not only a cryptocurrency network.

90
00:06:53,290 --> 00:06:59,801
It is a programmable settlement layer for digital ownership, money, coordination, and applications.

91
00:06:59,801 --> 00:07:02,939
I am Lucas Bispo de Oliveira.

92
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:05,074
See you on-chain.
